    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d791">

                <pb facs="0978" n="854" sameAs="frus:frus1921v01/pg854"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">839.51/2166</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of the Navy</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Daniels</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Acting
                        Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1920-12-21"><hi rendition="#italic">December 21,
                        1920</hi></date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">Op–13</seg>
                    <lb/>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">16870–315:11</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to forward herewith a
                    communication from the Military Governor of Santo Domingo,<anchor n="30"
                        xml:id="d791-f1"/> requesting the consent of the United States Government to
                    increase the public debt of the Dominican Republic in the amount of ten million
                    dollars pursuant to the provisions of the American-Dominican Convention of 1907
                    providing for the assistance of the United States in the collection and
                    application of the customs revenues of the Dominican Republic, under date of
                    February 8, 1907.<anchor n="31" xml:id="d791-f2"/></p>
                <p>The Navy Department recommends the approval of this request, as it is considered
                    that the public works which have been started by the Military Government as
                    essential to the commercial and educational development of the Dominican
                    Republic, should be continued as projected in the interests of the people of
                    Dominica irrespective of any arrangements that may be made as to the future
                    administration of the government of that Republic. It is, of course, assumed
                    that the United States will continue such control of the financial
                    administration of the Dominican Republic as will insure that the proceeds of the
                    proposed loan will be properly applied to the public works projected and also to
                    provide for the payment of the interest and principal of the proposed bond
                    issue.</p>
                <p>Yours truly,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Josephus Daniels</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
